          "question": "I get short of breath climbing two flights of stairs but I am completely fine at rest.",
          "answer": "The tool did not flag this as an emergency. It also did not clear it. Its note says that a symptom missing from its list can still be serious, so it is worth raising with a doctor, especially if the breathlessness is new or getting worse.\n\nThe retrieved guidance does not explain what causes breathlessness on exertion. The closest passage is from the \"Staying active with COPD\" note (lifestyle-copd-activity). It describes breathing techniques used during effort, such as pursed-lip breathing and pacing breath with movement, and says leaning forward on a rail can help recovery. That note is about managing a known lung condition, and nothing here shows that it applies to you. The \"Physical activity for high blood pressure\" note (lifestyle-hypertension-activity) says that short bouts of stair climbing count toward activity, but it does not address breathlessness.\n\nPlease seek medical care promptly, or call emergency services, if you have any of these:\n- Chest pain, pressure, or tightness\n- Breathlessness that comes on at rest, or gets worse quickly\n- Fainting, near-fainting, or a racing or irregular heartbeat\n- Blue or grey lips, or confusion\n- Swelling in the legs, or waking up short of breath\n\nFor a non-urgent question, a doctor can ask about how long this has been happening, whether it has changed, and what else is going on, then decide what tests make sense.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
